{"@context":{"lean":"lean:","block":"block:","imports":{"@type":"@id"}},"@graph":[{"@id":"lean:module:QOU.MathlibExt.QuadraticIrrationalBadlyApproximable","@type":"LeanModule","name":"QOU.MathlibExt.QuadraticIrrationalBadlyApproximable","imports":["lean:module:Init","lean:module:Mathlib.RingTheory.Real.Irrational","lean:module:Mathlib.Algebra.Order.Round","lean:module:Mathlib.Algebra.Order.Group.Abs"],"declarations":3,"chunks":1},{"@id":"lean:QOU.MathlibExt.quadratic_irrational_fract_badlyApproximable","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.MathlibExt.quadratic_irrational_fract_badlyApproximable","kind":"theorem","line":184,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/MathlibExt/QuadraticIrrationalBadlyApproximable.lean#L184-L263","doc":"**Every real quadratic irrational is badly approximable (fract form).**  If `β > 0`\nis irrational and satisfies an integer quadratic `Aβ² + Bβ + C = 0` (`A ≠ 0`), then\n`∃ κ > 0, ∀ j ≥ 1, κ/j ≤ {jβ}` — the degree-2 Liouville bound.  With `p = ⌊jβ⌋`, the\ninteger `N = A p² + B p j + C j²` is nonzero (else `p/j` is a rational root, forcing\n`β` or its conjugate rational), so `|N| ≥ 1`; the identity\n`N = −(jβ − p)·(A(p+jβ) + Bj)` (using the root relation) with the bound\n`|A(p+jβ)+Bj| ≤ j·(2|A|β + |B|)` gives `{jβ} = jβ − p ≥ 1/(j·(2|A|β+|B|))`.\nGeneralizes `sqrt_fract_badlyApproximable` (`A=1, B=0, C=−D`) to all quadratic\nsurds, hence the unconditional Dilog Part B limit to `x = e^{−β}` for every\nquadratic-irrational `β > 0`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.MathlibExt.sqrt_fract_badlyApproximable","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.MathlibExt.sqrt_fract_badlyApproximable","kind":"theorem","line":116,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/MathlibExt/QuadraticIrrationalBadlyApproximable.lean#L116-L182","doc":"**Fractional-part form.**  For `α = √D` irrational, `c/j ≤ {jα}` for all `j ≥ 1`\n(`{·} = Int.fract`).  This is the shape the Dilog Part B maximal-cell branch-point\ngap needs (the gap is `≈ {j√D}·(q_j − 1)`).  Proved directly with `p = ⌊jα⌋`: since\n`p ≤ jα`, `jα + p ≤ 2αj` (no `1/2` slack), and `D j² − p²` is a positive integer\n(`{jα} = 0` would make `jα = p ∈ ℤ`, contradicting irrationality), so\n`{jα} = jα − p = (D j² − p²)/(jα + p) ≥ 1/(2αj)`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.MathlibExt.sqrt_badlyApproximable","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.MathlibExt.sqrt_badlyApproximable","kind":"theorem","line":30,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/MathlibExt/QuadraticIrrationalBadlyApproximable.lean#L30-L114","doc":"**Quadratic surds are badly approximable.**  For `α = √D` irrational there is\n`c > 0` with `c/j ≤ |jα − round(jα)|` for all `j ≥ 1`.  Elementary: with\n`p = round(jα)`, the integer `D j² − p²` is nonzero (else `α = p/j` is rational),\nso `|D j² − p²| ≥ 1`; factoring `(jα − p)(jα + p) = D j² − p²` and bounding\n`|jα + p| ≤ (2α + 1)·j` gives `|jα − p| ≥ 1/((2α+1) j)`. ","signatureChunk":0}]}